course: dictionary
lesson: 11533
title: Aloof: keeping your distance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Aloof means unfriendly and distant. Someone who is aloof keeps away from other people and does not join in. Clues in the sentence help you spot it: «Mia sat alone at the far end of the bench and did not speak to anyone.» Mia is keeping away from the others, so aloof fits.
explain: Aloof can also mean keeping apart from something on purpose. «Sam stayed aloof from the argument and read his book.» Sam did not take a side, so aloof means he chose not to get involved.
explain: Try the swap check. Put a word such as distant or unfriendly in place of aloof. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning. Writers often use aloof for a character who seems proud or shy, so the reader wonders why they keep to themselves.
fact: Aloof means unfriendly and distant, keeping away from other people.
fact: «Keep aloof from» means to stay apart from something on purpose.
fact: «Remain aloof» means to stay distant and not join in.
fact: Writers often call cats aloof, because a cat seems to choose when to come close.
fact: Aloof is usually an adjective, as in «an aloof cat», but it can also be an adverb, as in «stand aloof».
exercise: «The king stayed aloof from the crowd and never smiled.» What does aloof mean here? | Lost in the crowd | Cheerful and loud | Wet from the rain | * Distant and unfriendly
exercise: «Sam kept aloof from the argument.» What does kept aloof suggest? | Ran away from home | Joined in with both sides | Shouted the loudest | * Did not get involved
exercise: Priya says aloof means very hungry. Is she right? | Yes, it means lost and cold | Yes, it means very hungry | Yes, it means very tired | * No, it means distant and unfriendly
test: «The new boy stood aloof at the edge of the playground.» What does aloof mean? | * Keeping away from the others | Asleep on the grass | Running very fast | Eager to play
test: Which phrase means to stay apart from something on purpose? | * Stay aloof from | Jump in with | Laugh at | Hurry up to
exam: «Even though she was invited, Ruby kept aloof from the party and sat in the corner.» Why does the author use aloof? | To show the party was very loud | To show Ruby ran a race | To say Ruby was very hungry | * To show Ruby kept her distance from the party
